[{:db/id "q-345095/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "cff9aa8a-9cde-574e-b1c9-74009357eb2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345095/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f036e760-5e7a-5e10-b5c8-46509a713fa8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345095/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-345095/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345095" :question/id #uuid "e683e6e6-51b8-5bab-bea4-dfa410c95734" :question/math-academy-id "q-345095" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{10}{13})}^{2}+{(\\frac{24}{13})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{676}{169}}=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345095/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345095"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345120/field-1/81a24a13f7226a3f1346d5ab1ec9a842425743879b1eb294da0e4c48b312d1c6" :answer/id #uuid "e3583c34-e19e-5659-99e0-ab659428c8b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12e^{-3t}+12e^{-6t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345120/field-1/d6ed4f75603c793cae60574e5a8f6e65813941f1a1cbb9d3193bd2bfd1504007" :answer/id #uuid "672b1fc5-849a-5b40-9f44-780bca352280" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4e^{-3t}-2e^{-6t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345120/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3e7b6ae8-84db-5e82-9b74-56dbc3d84c65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345120/field-1/81a24a13f7226a3f1346d5ab1ec9a842425743879b1eb294da0e4c48b312d1c6" :answer-field/choices ["q-345120/field-1/81a24a13f7226a3f1346d5ab1ec9a842425743879b1eb294da0e4c48b312d1c6" "q-345120/field-1/d6ed4f75603c793cae60574e5a8f6e65813941f1a1cbb9d3193bd2bfd1504007"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345120" :question/id #uuid "d9b75dc7-e664-55bf-ad97-8cdf63faee64" :question/math-academy-id "q-345120" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-3t}+12{e}^{-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-345120/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345120"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345088/field-1/b0ef4b9ed0ed509d34bf60e309bf174c549406cec6e3889539cb8d90bf677043" :answer/id #uuid "62d8b86f-962b-51b4-b133-e25dc481b42c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8e^{-4t}-5e^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345088/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f77f9e0-5149-5082-9006-6fff2a844abf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345088/field-1/b0ef4b9ed0ed509d34bf60e309bf174c549406cec6e3889539cb8d90bf677043" :answer-field/choices ["q-345088/field-1/b0ef4b9ed0ed509d34bf60e309bf174c549406cec6e3889539cb8d90bf677043"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345088" :question/id #uuid "02e0e7d3-1b8f-51ea-ad6d-6c1ff89eac20" :question/math-academy-id "q-345088" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-4t}+5{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{17}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-4t}+5{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{17}) \\\\  & =-5{e}^{-t}+8{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-345088/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345088"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345096/field-1/42d955a272f7d7c4332aa26375222e91de1afe30b2989c800106c5625bb910aa" :answer/id #uuid "00f98591-30d5-5838-a78f-1deda44e33ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{65}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345096/field-1/313ffc45185ac67b3a88c464787a0cf11a04a5ab2d07152c554b8bb7d385533d" :answer/id #uuid "be935e69-aadc-5afb-a3d1-108121f1449a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345096/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8374f5e8-c25e-54ed-a1fb-bd4e14bbd9d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345096/field-1/42d955a272f7d7c4332aa26375222e91de1afe30b2989c800106c5625bb910aa" :answer-field/choices ["q-345096/field-1/42d955a272f7d7c4332aa26375222e91de1afe30b2989c800106c5625bb910aa" "q-345096/field-1/313ffc45185ac67b3a88c464787a0cf11a04a5ab2d07152c554b8bb7d385533d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345096" :question/id #uuid "9aa505e2-e827-5766-9e0e-4192e921e162" :question/math-academy-id "q-345096" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{60}{17})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{25}{17})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4225}{289}}=\\frac{65}{17}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345096/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345096"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345094/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "621cee02-6cec-5df5-bec8-8f603e8ca865" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345094/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "747b2cb0-c6da-5277-87aa-8055ec36a502" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345094/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-345094/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345094" :question/id #uuid "387ad333-2ae7-53ce-b369-30dcd456b707" :question/math-academy-id "q-345094" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)={e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{36+64}{25}}=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345094/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345094"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345115/selection/e38859be815c4b31c533e0a469ed5bf8ece39ac5dbc446d7819a4a0837bdaf5f" :answer/id #uuid "0e3b00a6-891d-59d1-8df1-d5f7a1760ffe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345115/selection/f8f2df86d63f070c97fc73a13cca1ed268f6761cda0fd3de3e7de69f4b2896a2" :answer/id #uuid "9b59beaa-65b4-5702-9dad-4f2484025c81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345115/selection/30c5dfe58b66f8ab2a1d7e33f64f8df5e62ebfb7ee8125751f6449f6ea2340e0" :answer/id #uuid "a11a9b1c-dc7c-5725-aea7-2b03328b8e42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345115/selection/259635861548ba52c8f19b5930f478154dba138bd1647968297b77d6a12d63e6" :answer/id #uuid "d8025af6-6f05-5c00-b639-0b57d479831d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345115/selection/7ecb03da70cb3562f7074182804e2ddd37f63965a647af04ddde008f09c97797" :answer/id #uuid "f0a12df2-4d9b-5bf1-aa1c-7c0eda7c1b18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345115/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "69917647-b198-5f68-b57f-3a2e295faad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345115/selection/e38859be815c4b31c533e0a469ed5bf8ece39ac5dbc446d7819a4a0837bdaf5f" :answer-field/choices ["q-345115/selection/e38859be815c4b31c533e0a469ed5bf8ece39ac5dbc446d7819a4a0837bdaf5f" "q-345115/selection/f8f2df86d63f070c97fc73a13cca1ed268f6761cda0fd3de3e7de69f4b2896a2" "q-345115/selection/30c5dfe58b66f8ab2a1d7e33f64f8df5e62ebfb7ee8125751f6449f6ea2340e0" "q-345115/selection/259635861548ba52c8f19b5930f478154dba138bd1647968297b77d6a12d63e6" "q-345115/selection/7ecb03da70cb3562f7074182804e2ddd37f63965a647af04ddde008f09c97797"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345115" :question/id #uuid "407ff137-25ea-5a9b-9dfe-5bb8913d57e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-345115" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/8cc2afe50a1f1e3ed0a173cdba6f5a5f59145b0afa6038bee7c9a1fe720238a6.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.125\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345115/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e5f77be-ef08-568f-8c7c-cae99d23beed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345115"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345105/selection/f32615ec2dbf9ff0de7fd51c2374847cb510169bc19046c99a5571056d6977d7" :answer/id #uuid "aa438c34-8472-546d-8818-9254eff15de3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345105/selection/2c201f536709a85eba6f5cb9fb3a43006525ca8c110584511c859aba6a1cf5a2" :answer/id #uuid "ef69b763-3bde-5d6b-aff2-8194abebfb08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345105/selection/15d95f1f21f853b8fd4a32b3548ebe0c22535370237649b77e70c9f7aadd1f51" :answer/id #uuid "c0c1efa6-29a9-5996-b108-12d506567bca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{2}q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345105/selection/1cd6804bac0ef9619387664cb199fcd47f8957bacd2c87a3be3d6c68befde520" :answer/id #uuid "2ffaf9a0-7276-5131-a3ed-809ce4bdec52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345105/selection/148a3c9fbf1837d0932defcf1fb88e1ff7dad3006ec3462c65b95ec4ca7ecd47" :answer/id #uuid "2620791e-c52a-54c7-bd62-e595f17a9c2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345105/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bacc07b3-c950-5470-84e0-d567e26484c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345105/selection/f32615ec2dbf9ff0de7fd51c2374847cb510169bc19046c99a5571056d6977d7" :answer-field/choices ["q-345105/selection/f32615ec2dbf9ff0de7fd51c2374847cb510169bc19046c99a5571056d6977d7" "q-345105/selection/2c201f536709a85eba6f5cb9fb3a43006525ca8c110584511c859aba6a1cf5a2" "q-345105/selection/15d95f1f21f853b8fd4a32b3548ebe0c22535370237649b77e70c9f7aadd1f51" "q-345105/selection/1cd6804bac0ef9619387664cb199fcd47f8957bacd2c87a3be3d6c68befde520" "q-345105/selection/148a3c9fbf1837d0932defcf1fb88e1ff7dad3006ec3462c65b95ec4ca7ecd47"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345105" :question/id #uuid "e91d1cc3-5fea-561e-9d55-806e9a7ba596" :question/math-academy-id "q-345105" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $15\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/a051a6380df51ba3c1141bc69952ba442dc9c816df30c5640846894fe1ba4c31.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=15\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=15I=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345105/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e5f77be-ef08-568f-8c7c-cae99d23beed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345105"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345112/selection/063a87276ab7a13966de032e3388338c318aa60f18b17160513e6840aa8dc87c" :answer/id #uuid "87e81d6c-029b-57f5-83a1-c34716616bcd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345112/selection/4a422b216a1043be940a92a544ccc3d5cd5e76cb49a2f07075bfcadd28234662" :answer/id #uuid "6ebcc95e-77f6-5432-9714-fd8ec1dfc1be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.08q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345112/selection/29b5687a6235d3a0011ad6cbc95a4394099913d9d1f4161a09c914ef65a46434" :answer/id #uuid "4cd51165-a2e8-531a-809b-be333bcb7ada" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345112/selection/4f490e5b5ff02717d036d1b8ad2cc9c3b1da6250541a637a62b0b006417bd5a2" :answer/id #uuid "2c55c7a1-ef2c-5621-b915-ad2ff3af5ae1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345112/selection/6f938a7dd7ba92e60e88fb9f01153579d1d30db07bf4660d6d4eb01e45ceab52" :answer/id #uuid "437f91e7-5307-55cd-a963-045598663f33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.08q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345112/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ffea2ec-8de5-5235-af25-3729e2c1986f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345112/selection/063a87276ab7a13966de032e3388338c318aa60f18b17160513e6840aa8dc87c" :answer-field/choices ["q-345112/selection/063a87276ab7a13966de032e3388338c318aa60f18b17160513e6840aa8dc87c" "q-345112/selection/4a422b216a1043be940a92a544ccc3d5cd5e76cb49a2f07075bfcadd28234662" "q-345112/selection/29b5687a6235d3a0011ad6cbc95a4394099913d9d1f4161a09c914ef65a46434" "q-345112/selection/4f490e5b5ff02717d036d1b8ad2cc9c3b1da6250541a637a62b0b006417bd5a2" "q-345112/selection/6f938a7dd7ba92e60e88fb9f01153579d1d30db07bf4660d6d4eb01e45ceab52"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345112" :question/id #uuid "5d7a291b-5fc4-538e-a7c3-a6ff291892c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-345112" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/a73d5f0c60062001e947301760fe1cdae7e0e17c7fcc9870baf9d0262070b238.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.025\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.025}=40q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+40q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-345112/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e5f77be-ef08-568f-8c7c-cae99d23beed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345112"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345103/selection/932e55a8c9132aef40ab9e8283e458a6bc22bdc1214461b4a66b326842e7a491" :answer/id #uuid "9afcf8e8-5326-50a0-a1a7-1cd55f2cdb6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345103/selection/935ed2d7e5198f3c7c85d824a5d2401c1a59fe40978999d7bb3d7327ea3a2f3c" :answer/id #uuid "3bd9e059-04a4-5633-b1e5-d36697d804dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345103/selection/61b56ae401fd6723079315052ea6c55572f0b017815e9eaffce6d1b9695959d3" :answer/id #uuid "d4beda4a-abef-5700-8c93-44ec3a8e477c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345103/selection/063096e0e681fcbf81e5434f0c0e57cb7cbc94d8171feb8de2a1ebc25ffa5679" :answer/id #uuid "f6f558e2-fab8-5863-8f5b-ec84ab9a5a32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345103/selection/a109e7c0e6df85156997b606e636eee1e64548884fb3fe73c2222ce22f312a2d" :answer/id #uuid "1777ce80-4949-575c-9601-32784d2ba86a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345103/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a268e6af-30b3-58c9-835c-003904af5fad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345103/selection/a109e7c0e6df85156997b606e636eee1e64548884fb3fe73c2222ce22f312a2d" :answer-field/choices ["q-345103/selection/932e55a8c9132aef40ab9e8283e458a6bc22bdc1214461b4a66b326842e7a491" "q-345103/selection/935ed2d7e5198f3c7c85d824a5d2401c1a59fe40978999d7bb3d7327ea3a2f3c" "q-345103/selection/61b56ae401fd6723079315052ea6c55572f0b017815e9eaffce6d1b9695959d3" "q-345103/selection/063096e0e681fcbf81e5434f0c0e57cb7cbc94d8171feb8de2a1ebc25ffa5679" "q-345103/selection/a109e7c0e6df85156997b606e636eee1e64548884fb3fe73c2222ce22f312a2d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345103" :question/id #uuid "baba8f77-9934-50a4-96a3-21fe5cd03d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-345103" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/6494f1905ab90cb3d5655aaa824726255dc7332841ec656dc88c2180b0f93a1c.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345103/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e5f77be-ef08-568f-8c7c-cae99d23beed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345103"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-343714"] :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/169844e6d45393119ac1579cc89e34850401810bfcc1c7537d5b72851a0b2b02.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=20\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-345130/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "12b2443e-9d72-56eb-b1bb-d6ef542dedea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345130/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6886257a-b23f-5396-81f8-b946865fe975" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345130/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-345130/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345130" :question/id #uuid "6b4773ff-8f02-5bdd-8f58-602b05f01a07" :question/math-academy-id "q-345130" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/50bb98a563a0ee061a0faa4d4390aaf459a2fdfccb4721d5d7e6ddd3102c98b1.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I=12\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+12\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=6.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n4(0)+12(0)+4q=6,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n4q=6\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{2}$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-345130/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345130"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345127/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "83fe316d-340f-5059-82a9-d59892396090" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345127/field-1/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" :answer/id #uuid "a4d310d2-2260-5271-b612-b3e8a700a3c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345127/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7d2e104-3591-57b8-b21f-04d8fee6197e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345127/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-345127/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-345127/field-1/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345127" :question/id #uuid "a75841e4-1687-53dc-8746-b00a6bfb5621" :question/math-academy-id "q-345127" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=6\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/d7145f4b394e7675b87ed851e344756ecb8441ce737f8664fbfd4b0e426ef36f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/10)}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=6\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=6\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=6\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n6\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=15.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n6(0)+30(0)+10q=15,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n10q=15\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{2}$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345127/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345127"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345122/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "39ecd252-125d-53b6-b1da-c6f3d5fbf1c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345122/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "94423fcd-91fa-58b7-a231-00bbd913c6cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345122/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-345122/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345122" :question/id #uuid "8915f1df-76e2-5206-9997-c56b044c99b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-345122" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0,\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the steady-state charge, we substitute these values into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24\n$$\n\nSubstituting the steady-state conditions gives\n\n$$\n0+4(0)+8q=24,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q=24\\,⟹\\,q=3.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current $I=\\frac{dq}{dt}$ has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $3$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-345122/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345122"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345015/field-1/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" :answer/id #uuid "68f11e44-cb94-5ee0-853a-a5f803a98cd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345015/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "6fb4eed5-a18a-5485-ae0a-df96f6b9f71a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186118130 :answer-field/correct "q-345015/field-1/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" :answer-field/choices ["q-345015/field-1/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" "q-345015/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-345015"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/b885a0dc4bb868284ec96bcbfc964c767fc159aa0728044d722f0f6b11cbfb1f.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $14\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=14\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=14I=14\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+14\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=8.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n4(0)+14(0)+10q=8,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n10q=8\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{8}{10}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{4}{5}$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-345014/field-1/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e" :answer/id #uuid "cb46788b-3698-51e7-8519-5f9881a9a812" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186118129 :answer-field/correct "q-345014/field-1/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e" :answer-field/choices ["q-345014/field-1/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-345014"] :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $34\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/d8303b679d4e00bac0f59f51941a2842473e050033f87f56f099b853431ac5aa.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=34\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=34I=34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/24)}=24q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=12\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+24q=9.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n12(0)+34(0)+24q=9,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n24q=9\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{9}{24}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{8}$ coulombs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-345135/selection/a704a47a9643541880a3f1f98e171382fbd1d302b32e283222778778a227182b" :answer/id #uuid "02b4086e-2a6d-507f-99a6-020db1d758e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.86" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345135/selection/909930bebd0471aaf20a341ddcc13d3017d1cf6a52c3019574196a3def636dee" :answer/id #uuid "d033742a-b227-52ca-957e-1e4ba1fa4339" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345135/selection/973440a4e49c02597a47b55d877c9f18a99196e9104381d471be63b9533ff2eb" :answer/id #uuid "6573958d-375d-5136-9a00-e827c05e4894" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.71" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345135/selection/bd5782f747e7369c0e06f70e9fb966d8f5da43f00ba26bb45de56407f5a57102" :answer/id #uuid "bc2795db-6e14-54f8-9924-1d6afa85512a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.00" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345135/selection/867645cc65f2523d2da0f0bf9f79dcff4e4758e45f7d0d53126cc6ed4f8a9875" :answer/id #uuid "5f3cc937-e5e9-5d39-aed4-02311866025f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345135/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c60b745-20d7-5d62-b947-bdb19b4cc34b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345135/selection/973440a4e49c02597a47b55d877c9f18a99196e9104381d471be63b9533ff2eb" :answer-field/choices ["q-345135/selection/a704a47a9643541880a3f1f98e171382fbd1d302b32e283222778778a227182b" "q-345135/selection/909930bebd0471aaf20a341ddcc13d3017d1cf6a52c3019574196a3def636dee" "q-345135/selection/973440a4e49c02597a47b55d877c9f18a99196e9104381d471be63b9533ff2eb" "q-345135/selection/bd5782f747e7369c0e06f70e9fb966d8f5da43f00ba26bb45de56407f5a57102" "q-345135/selection/867645cc65f2523d2da0f0bf9f79dcff4e4758e45f7d0d53126cc6ed4f8a9875"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345135" :question/id #uuid "201a8e5a-fe9e-5acf-a68d-5d850bdb2777" :question/math-academy-id "q-345135" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/1146917884634beea990c281a591257898887a11029f0116e10f8bfd38169282.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $15\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=15\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=15I=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=15\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+2{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+3(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\, &  \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{7}{10},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{7}{10})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}≈0.71,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345135/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5db2c6fe-1b26-5daa-b281-d6f6481dc268"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345135"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345144/selection/acc37fb19f08fe8a56374b1ef3a5204ebde288c64ffb3eef547ced9efd63b7a5" :answer/id #uuid "73a0cff8-44c2-5e03-9a5f-cda0ab7b3042" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.58" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345144/selection/2e87a24a01de4df4c5084615e733d737d26bf1cd3fe678410da7aff8cbf45301" :answer/id #uuid "8c62e345-9929-551e-92ed-c90da75f99af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345144/selection/6c3fe5936b48b6895a0cca907277410fe16fa6a16cf280ebb305d391a97cd1f3" :answer/id #uuid "3e1b1cca-a4c0-5d03-992e-be878db75e4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345144/selection/bd0c5293c05dbcd56341c67931e9e0ae46e20e1d0de3da140e1b2dcd87ea42e2" :answer/id #uuid "9355363c-932c-53e3-9dae-0547125e5987" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345144/selection/9a919a5dd55ee179d5a939b66417540c77a1da1b49a565b4a9ba049471fc60c4" :answer/id #uuid "8de0e4b2-a008-5b95-8d7f-5932890ebf1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345144/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4086525-2622-595a-aafe-640b32bf6321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345144/selection/bd0c5293c05dbcd56341c67931e9e0ae46e20e1d0de3da140e1b2dcd87ea42e2" :answer-field/choices ["q-345144/selection/acc37fb19f08fe8a56374b1ef3a5204ebde288c64ffb3eef547ced9efd63b7a5" "q-345144/selection/2e87a24a01de4df4c5084615e733d737d26bf1cd3fe678410da7aff8cbf45301" "q-345144/selection/6c3fe5936b48b6895a0cca907277410fe16fa6a16cf280ebb305d391a97cd1f3" "q-345144/selection/bd0c5293c05dbcd56341c67931e9e0ae46e20e1d0de3da140e1b2dcd87ea42e2" "q-345144/selection/9a919a5dd55ee179d5a939b66417540c77a1da1b49a565b4a9ba049471fc60c4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345144" :question/id #uuid "4f2fdcab-4043-5d2d-8933-45a208180ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-345144" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/42ab81638355355340008eaa54657a0144612ec9d04e44266350cdb8012051de.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-41\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+6(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-18\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{41}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +18\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha -5\\beta =-\\frac{41}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{(-3+\\sqrt{5})t}+B{e}^{(-3-\\sqrt{5})t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}≈0.45,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345144/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5db2c6fe-1b26-5daa-b281-d6f6481dc268"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345144"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-343948"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/00602c631bee877e48211dd94a6ea6e1bbb26626f028212c1388ed547f2452f8.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $105\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{180}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $15\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=105\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=105I=105\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/180)}=180q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=15\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=15\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=15\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=105\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+180q+15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+12q=\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+12{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-21\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(3\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-21\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +21\\beta =\\frac{10}{3}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{45},$ $\\beta =\\frac{7}{45}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-3B{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-3B{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{15})}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{15})}^{2}}≈0.47,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-345132/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "cfb873cf-66f2-5cbc-80b8-9f1121b0d9ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345132/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "afeedda3-15ea-5e25-bbde-7663febf6786" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345132/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "8ae685b0-f9bd-572e-9fd9-e83f9126cba4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345132/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "db7a726b-f7e3-5740-ac98-132dc67fd3c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345132/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "7046bfe2-c8fb-5d46-b804-9a8d105b5e21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345132/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6ca543-dcfb-5845-9b98-826034062223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345132/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-345132/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" "q-345132/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-345132/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-345132/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-345132/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345132" :question/id #uuid "61885c5a-2af5-5794-bf2c-8ceb10ff2a25" :question/math-academy-id "q-345132" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/7cbcdf7d43a99b9558b1d085d2306c22e1d27134a5b0ea428721ce7bd074b725.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{P}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=5\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=5I=5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q+\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+5(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (3\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-5\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -5\\alpha +3\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{3}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-5,$ $Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-5+3=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-345132/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5db2c6fe-1b26-5daa-b281-d6f6481dc268"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345132"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-343807"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/c8600d487acea6755d032a17a04c1eb584bc1a84888af5ac152001a4a03aa8b1.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $70\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=70\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=70I=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-21\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-21\\alpha +\\beta =\\frac{39}{10}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ \\alpha +21\\beta =-\\frac{13}{2}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{5},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{3}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{10})}^{2}}≈0.36,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21893"] :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t) = 4e^{-3t} - 2e^{-t} + \\dfrac5{17}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac3{17}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-t}+\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-3t}+2{e}^{-t}-\\frac{10}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{6}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = -12e^{-3t} + 2e^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{10}{17}\\sin(2t) + \\dfrac{6}{17}\\cos(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{10}{17}\\sin(2t) + \\dfrac{6}{17}\\cos(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{10}{17}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{6}{17}\\right)^2}\n= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{136}{289}}\n= \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{34}}{17}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21890"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/649a710caaad131ccf79589e91d6059d8be55f2eb86230be6d3b44313a5c733d.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit shown in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The capacitor charge and voltage satisfy $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance (in Farads).*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\cos(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $18\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\dfrac1{48}\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $6\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 18I = 18\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/48)} = 48q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 6\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9\\cos(3t) = 18\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 48q + 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $6,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 8q = \\dfrac32\\cos(3t).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21891"] :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitance of $\\dfrac{1}{20}\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductance of $L=10\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The capacitor charge and voltage satisfy $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is given below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/2f7e02ba534c41bec3783c56a5ce1b94422757f4398af0098db5ba9a0d6ec49e.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/20)} = 20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10 = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 1.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t \\to \\infty.$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n(0)+ 3(0) + 2q = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n2q = 1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\dfrac12$ coulombs." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21892"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/94d6100570bdcf98de739edfa18e68e5ba6889ce0ba7cd573cdb07c2cad68b33.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.05} = 20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(4t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 2\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-16\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+3(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) &  \\\\ \\,+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-14\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+(-12\\alpha -14\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-14\\alpha +12\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -14\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{6}{85}$ and $\\beta = -\\dfrac{7}{85}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t} - \\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{6}{85}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\dfrac{7}{85}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.11,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
